"Internal Error ()\nciteturn6500view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15894v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15894v1: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6500view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15977v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15977v1: (404) Not Found\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCase Prompting to Mitigate Large Language Model Bias for ICU Mortality Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19735v1)\nciteturn6500view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19735v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 333\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15: \nL16: cite5†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L17: \nL18: arXiv:2512.19735v1 [cs.LG] 17 Dec 2025\nL19: # Case Prompting to Mitigate Large Language Model Bias for ICU Mortality Prediction\nL20: Gangxiong Zhang Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, Tianjin University of Technology, Tianjin, China Affiliation: National Institute of Health Data Science, Peking University, Beijing, China    Yongchao Long Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, Tianjin University of Technology, Tianjin, China Affiliation: National Institute of Health Data Science, Peking University, Beijing, China    Yong Zhang Affiliation: BNRist, Intelligent Database Research Institute, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China Affiliation: Correspondence: joy_yuxi@pku.edu.cn, hongshenda@pku.edu.cn, zhangyong05@tsinghua.edu.cn    Yuxi Zhou Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, Tianjin University of Technology, Tianjin, China Affiliation: DCST, BNRist, RIIT, Institute of Internet Industry, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China Affiliation: Correspondence: joy_yuxi@pku.edu.cn, hongshenda@pku.edu.cn, zhangyong05@tsinghua.edu.cn    Shenda Hong Affiliation: National Institute of Health Data Science, Peking University, Beijing, China Affiliation: Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Peking University, Beijing, China Affiliation: Correspondence: joy_yuxi@pku.edu.cn, hongshenda@pku.edu.cn, zhangyong05@tsinghua.edu.cn\nL21: ## ABSTRACT\nL22: Accurately predicting mortality risk in intensive care unit (ICU) patients is critical for clinical decision-making^{cite6†35 }. While large language models (LLMs) show significant potential for prediction tasks using structured medical data, their decision-making processes may harbor biases related to demographic attributes such as sex, age, and race ^{cite7†10 }. This issue severely hampers the trustworthy deployment of LLMs in clinical settings where fairness is paramount.\nL23: Existing bias mitigation approaches often compromise model performance, making it challenging to achieve a synergistic optimization of fairness and predictive accuracy. This study systematically investigates fairness issues in LLMs applied to ICU mortality prediction and proposes a clinically adaptive prompting framework. The framework is designed to enhance both model fairness and performance simultaneously via a low-cost, training-free approach.\nL24: We first construct a multi-dimensional bias assessment system for comprehensive model diagnosis. Subsequently, we design a framework named CAse Prompting (CAP), which not only incorporates existing prompt-based debiasing techniques but also pioneers a new direction by guiding the model to learn from similar historical misprediction cases alongside their correct outcomes, thereby correcting biased reasoning patterns.\nL25: Experiments on the MIMIC-IV dataset demonstrate that our CAP framework achieves significant improvements in both performance and fairness metrics. CAP increases the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) from 0.806 to 0.873 (+0.067) and the area under the precision-recall curve (AUPRC) from 0.497 to 0.694 (+0.197), while reducing sex- and race-related prediction disparities by over 90%.\nL26: Feature reliance analysis confirms that the model maintains highly consistent attention patterns across demographic groups, with key similarity metrics exceeding 0.98. This study confirms the presence of measurable bias in LLMs for ICU prediction and demonstrates that a carefully designed prompting framework can co-optimize fairness and performance without retraining, while providing a novel, transferable paradigm for developing reliable and equitable clinical decision-support tools.\nL27: ## KEYWORDS\nL28: \nL29: Large Language Models, ICU, Mortality Prediction, Fairness, Bias Mitigation, Prompt Engineering\nL30: ## INTRODUCTION\nL31: In high-risk clinical environments, concerns regarding the fairness of artificial intelligence (AI) systems have become a central ethical focus in both academia and industry^{cite8†16 }. As AI technologies become increasingly integrated into healthcare, their role in supporting decision-making in intensive care units (ICUs) has grown rapidly^{cite9†4 , cite10†17 , cite11†11 }.\nL32: Large language models (LLMs), with their advanced contextual understanding and reasoning capabilities, are emerging as powerful tools for clinical prediction tasks, including ICU mortality prediction. However, clinical decision-making requires not only high predictive accuracy but also stringent standards for fairness and interpretability.\nL33: Systematic performance disparities caused by non-clinical attributes—such as sex, race, or age—may lead to inequitable allocation of medical resources, exacerbate existing health disparities^{cite12†21 , cite13†33 }, and ultimately undermine clinicians’ and patients’ trust in AI-assisted care.\nL34: When Large Language Models (LLMs) are introduced into this high-risk clinical scenario, they face two key challenges. First, detecting demographic bias across patient subgroups. LLMs may inherit structural biases embedded in their training data, resulting in uneven performance for female, older, or minority patients^{cite14†26 }.\nL35: Conventional fairness metrics primarily measure output-level disparities but fail to capture latent feature bias—that is, the tendency of a model to rely on different clinical features for different demographic groups, thereby weakening model reliability. Second, how to break through the trade-off between performance and fairness, and mitigate model bias while ensuring performance.\nL36: Existing mitigation strategies often rely on reweighting or loss-based adjustments to improve fairness, yet these approaches frequently degrade overall accuracy—an outcome that is particularly problematic in high-stakes medical settings. Therefore, this study aims to systematically investigate the following three core research questions:\nL37:   * •\nL38: \nL39: Is bias present? Do LLMs indeed exhibit performance disparities across specific demographic groups in ICU mortality prediction?\nL40: \nL41:   * •\nL42: \nL43: Where is bias? Is bias confined to model outputs, or is it ingrained within the model’s internal feature reliance and reasoning logic (i.e., latent bias)?\nL44: \nL45:   * •\nL46: \nL47: Can bias be mitigated? Can these biases be mitigated in a low-cost, highly adaptable manner without compromising core predictive performance?\nL48: To address these questions, we put forward the core idea that by constructing a multi-dimensional bias assessment framework and designing a clinically adaptive prompting strategy, it is possible to co-optimize the fairness and predictive performance of LLMs without retraining. The main contributions of this work are as follows:\nL49: \nL50:   * •\nL51: We develop a multidimensional bias evaluation system that integrates model discriminative power, group fairness, and feature dependence analysis, enabling systematic detection and explanation of bias from \"output bias\" to \"reasoning bias\".\nL52: \nL53:   * •\nL54: We propose a clinically adaptive framework based on CAse prompting (CAP).By retrieving and incorporating similar historical misjudgment cases, this framework provides large language models (LLMs) with concrete decision-making contexts, guiding them to correct biased reasoning patterns and thereby improving both accuracy and fairness simultaneously.\nL55: \nL56:   * •\nL57: We conduct a full bias detection–mitigation–interpretation evaluation loop on the real-world MIMIC-IV clinical dataset, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed framework and offering a low-cost, generalizable pathway for the fair and trustworthy deployment of LLMs in clinical practice.\nL58: ## RESULTS\nL59: ### Study Population\nL60: This study utilized the MIMIC-IV database to construct a retrospective cohort. Prior to cohort establishment, data quality assessment and preprocessing were conducted: patients with excessively high missing data ratios were excluded; for partial missing values in the retained variables, the Multiple Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE) method was employed for data imputation to ensure data integrity and analytical reliability.\nL61: The inclusion criteria were defined as follows: adult patients admitted to the ICU for the first time with a hospital stay exceeding 24 hours. The final cohort comprised 45,173 patients, which were randomly divided into a training set (for constructing the case library and training comparative machine learning models) and an independent test set (for all final evaluations) at a 7:3 ratio.\nL62: The baseline characteristics of the patients are presented in Table cite15†1 , with balanced distributions of demographic and clinical characteristics across all subgroups.\nL63: Table 1: Baseline characteristics of the study cohort. SD, standard deviation. P-values were calculated using the t-test (for continuous variables) or the chi-square test (for categorical variables) comparing the training and test sets.\nL64: Feature  | Overall (n=45,153)  | Train (n=31,607)  | Test (n=13,546)  | p-value\nL65: Demographics\nL66: Age (years), mean ± SD  | 66.1 ± 16.2  | 66.1 ± 16.2  | 66.0 ± 16.2  | 0.12\nL67: Female, n (%)  | 20,138 (44.6)  | 14,072 (44.5)  | 6,066 (44.8)  | 0.62\nL68: Race, n (%)  |  |  |  | $<0.001$\nL69:    White  | 24,404 (54.1)  | 17,107 (54.1)  | 7,297 (53.9)  |\nL70:    Black  | 7,043 (15.6)  | 4,970 (15.7)  | 2,073 (15.3)  |\nL71:    Asian  | 512 (1.1)  | 358 (1.1)  | 154 (1.1)  |\nL72:    Other  | 13,194 (29.2)  | 9,172 (29.0)  | 4,022 (29.7)  |\nL73: Clinical Severity, mean ± SD\nL74: GCS score  | 8.0 ± 4.6  | 8.0 ± 4.6  | 8.0 ± 4.6  | 0.02\nL75: APACHE III score  | 29.3 ± 19.5  | 29.3 ± 19.6  | 29.2 ± 19.4  | 0.02\nL76: SOFA score (24 h)  | 3.9 ± 2.4  | 3.9 ± 2.4  | 3.9 ± 2.4  | 0.03\nL77: Charlson Comorbidity Index  | 5.1 ± 3.0  | 5.1 ± 3.0  | 5.1 ± 3.0  | $<0.001$\nL78: Vital Signs (first 24 hours), mean ± SD\nL79: SpO_{2,min} (%)  | 87.0 ± 4.4  | 87.0 ± 4.4  | 87.0 ± 4.4  | 0.65\nL80: Heart rate (beats/min)  | 87.8 ± 17.4  | 87.8 ± 17.5  | 87.7 ± 17.1  | 0.52\nL81: Respiratory rate (breaths/min)  | 19.3 ± 0.6  | 19.3 ± 0.6  | 19.3 ± 0.6  | 0.77\nL82: Mean arterial pressure (mmHg)  | 84.8 ± 2.9  | 84.8 ± 2.9  | 84.8 ± 3.0  | 0.39\nL83: Laboratory Measurements, mean ± SD\nL84: Creatinine_{max} (mg/dL)  | 3.2 ± 1.0  | 3.2 ± 1.0  | 3.2 ± 1.0  | 0.70\nL85: Lactate_{max} (mmol/L)  | 1.9 ± 0.4  | 1.9 ± 0.4  | 1.9 ± 0.4  | 0.40\nL86: Troponin_{max} (ng/mL)  | 0.8 ± 0.3  | 0.8 ± 0.3  |